# Language-Polished Final Version

Introduction

A Hysteresis-based authentication model that leverages temporal behavioural patterns and sensor fusion to achieve robust, low-overhead continuous authentication. 
The computational and statistical challenges, specifically the sensor noise (Veracity) and high-frequency state changes (Velocity), create an environment prone to the 'Ping-Pong Effect,' where a naive authentication system would rapidly toggle between Locked and Unlocked states. This failure mode precisely mirrors the instability faced by cellular networks at the cell edge. Therefore, to overcome these specific data characteristics, the research proposes adopting the Hard Handoff protocol from telecommunications. By implementing a Hysteresis-based decision engine (utilizing the Time-to-Trigger and Margin concepts), we aim to stabilize the authentication decision in this noisy, high-velocity environment, just as cellular networks stabilize signal transitions

To rigorously evaluate the proposed approach, this study utilizes the ExtraSensory Dataset. A dataset that captures (1) naturalistic, long-duration behaviour data; (2) multi-modal sensor streams from mobile and wearable devices; (3) sufficient temporal granularity to assess authentication decisions at sub-minute intervals; and (4) real-world noise and variability that stress-tests model robustness.

The ExtraSensory Dataset: Overview

The ExtraSensory Dataset, developed by the Context-Integrated Computing Lab at the University of California, San Diego, represents a comprehensive repository of behaviour context data collected from real-world mobile and wearable device usage . Unlike controlled laboratory datasets, ExtraSensory captures authentic user behaviour across diverse contexts, making it particularly suitable for evaluating authentication systems designed for real-world deployment.

Dataset Composition and Scale

The dataset contains sensor measurements and contextual labels collected from 60 participants over extended periods, resulting in more than 300,000 minutes (approximately 5,000 hours) of continuous data . Each participant contributed sensor readings from their personal smartphones and/or smartwatches during normal daily activities, ensuring ecological validity. The data collection methodology preserved natural behaviour patterns, including temporal variations, environmental changes, and the inherent unpredictability of real-world contexts.

Table 1 summarizes the core technical dimensions of the dataset:

Sensor Modalities and Feature Extraction

The dataset incorporates readings from multiple sensor types embedded in smartphones and smartwatches:

Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs): Accelerometer, gyroscope, and magnetometer data capturing device motion and orientation

Location Sensors: GPS coordinates and derived features (speed, heading)

Environmental Sensors: Ambient light, audio features (spectral characteristics, not raw audio for privacy)

Network Context: Wi-Fi and cellular connectivity information

Device State: Battery level, screen status, application usage patterns

From these raw sensor streams, researchers extracted 225 statistical and frequency-domain features per 20-second window, including mean, variance, spectral energy, zero-crossing rates, and correlation coefficients across sensor axes . This feature-level representation balances computational efficiency with information richness, making it suitable for real-time authentication systems.

Dataset Relevance to Hysteresis-Based Authentication

Alignment with Research Objectives

The ExtraSensory Dataset directly supports the evaluation of Hysteresis-based authentication through several key characteristics.

Temporal Granularity and Hysteresis Modelling: The ExtraSensory Dataset directly supports the evaluation of Hysteresis-based authentication by providing a temporal structure conducive to stability analysis. The dataset’s 20-second sampling interval offers sufficient granularity to model the temporal dynamics central to our proposed Time-to-Trigger (TTT) mechanism. Authentic behaviour is rarely static; it fluctuates. This dataset allows us to quantify the trade-off between responsiveness (detecting a new user quickly) and stability (ignoring transient anomalies), a balance that standard static-threshold models fail to achieve but which Hysteresis explicitly optimizes.

Multi-Modal Fusion Capabilities: Hysteresis-based authentication benefits significantly from the fusion of complementary behaviour signals. The dataset’s diverse sensor modalities (Magnetometer, Audio, WiFi) enable a rigorous investigation into how signal entropy varies across different physical domains. This allows us to tune the hysteresis parameters (e.g., the “Unlock Margin”) differently for a stable signal (Magnetometer) versus a volatile one (WiFi RSSI), thereby optimizing the overall Trust Score confidence.

Real-World Behaviour Variability: The naturalistic data collection captures the full spectrum of behaviour entropy that production systems must withstand: gradual behaviour drift, sudden context transitions, and erratic sensor noise. This uncontrolled environment provides a critical “Stress Test” for the proposed approach model, validating its robustness in scenarios where clean, laboratory-generated data would fail to expose the “Ping-Pong” locking vulnerabilities.

Benchmarking and Extensibility: This dataset serves as a standard benchmark for Context-Aware Computing, enabling the results of this research to be directly compared against existing baselines in the literature. Furthermore, the availability of deep raw sensor logs (IMU, Watch sensors) ensures the extensibility of this research. It permits future pivots toward “Gait Analysis” or “Cross-Device Handoff” (Watch-to-Phone) without necessitating a new data collection protocol, thus maximizing the long-term research value of the selected source.

Big Data Characteristics

Although the MSc Project utilizes the extracted feature data for benchmarking and computational feasibility, the entire/extended dataset fundamentally address the Big Data characteristics. The data could be present relevant scalability for continuous authentication systems and further sensor fusion studies.

Volume: While the extracted feature set is compressed for storage efficiency, it represents a massive temporal volume of over 300,000 minutes (over 5,000 hours) of continuous sensor logs. This longitudinal depth is critical; it allows for the evaluation of the Hysteresis algorithm over periods of days and weeks, rather than just seconds, ensuring that long-term concept drift is adequately modelled.

Velocity: The dataset originates from a high-velocity stream, where underlying sensors sample at rates between 40Hz (Motion) and 22kHz (Audio), aggregated into 20-second decision windows (Vaizman et al., 2017). In live deployments, the Handoff algorithm must achieve an inference latency of less than 1 second to avoid user-perceptible delays . This velocity constraint justifies the methodological choice of using lightweight feature vectors over raw waveforms, effectively reducing system load while maintaining real-time responsiveness.

Variety: The dataset exhibitss extreme heterogeneity across multiple dimensions, necessitating sophisticated fusion techniques. It fuses 225 heterogeneous features from 9 distinct modalities (including kinematic motion, acoustic pressure, and location), requiring complex alignment.

Veracity: Unlike clean laboratory datasets, this data was collected from users engaging in their usual real-life activities introducing significant veracity issues into the data, it contains significant noise and systematic missingness (16.5% missing data rate). It provides a rigorous stress and to ensure that any trained model will be stable even when the input data is unreliable such when in unpredictable, non-stationary environment.

