Justification of Non-Triviality

The ExtraSensory Dataset presents specific technical complexities that render standard “off-the-shelf” machine learning algorithms ineffective. These challenges necessitate the specialized Hysteresis-based modelling proposed in this research.

Feature Sparsity and Sensor Heterogeneity: Unlike standard tabular datasets where all features are present, this dataset suffers from systematic missingness. As noted by Vaizman et al. (2017), sensor availability varies by hardware (device heterogeneity) and user context (e.g., audio is unavailable during phone calls; GPS is disabled to save battery). Standard classifiers like SVMs, Random Forests cannot natively handle dynamic feature absence without introducing complex imputation strategies or utilizing robust multi-modal fusion architectures that can operate on partial vectors.

Selection Bias and Generalization Challenges: The dataset exhibitss significant demographic selection bias, with the majority of subjects being university students with age range of 18 – 42 years, with an average of 24.7 years old, this creates a Domain Shift problem. A standard model trained on this specific cohort will overfit to “student-like” behavioural patterns (irregular sleep schedules, campus mobility) and fail to generalize to a broader population. Overcoming this requires non-trivial techniques such as Transfer Learning or Domain Adaptation to ensure the authentication model remains valid for diverse user groups.

Class Imbalance (The “Student Routine” Bias): Due to the student centric population, the dataset is heavily skewed towards specific contexts such as “In Class” and “Lab Work” while under-representing others. To prevent the model from becoming biased toward these majority classes, standard training procedures must be augmented with sampling techniques such as SMOTE.

Question 2

Critical Analysis of Big Data Implementation Challenges

Practical implementation of the ExtraSensory dataset revealed significant computational and statistical challenges. The obstacles encountered during data ingestion and preprocessing further validate the non-trivial nature of the research problem, demonstrating that sophisticated engineering approaches were required beyond standard off-the-shelf machine learning pipelines. To facilitate reproducible, cloud-native analysis, the dataset was integrated into the Google Cloud ecosystem (utilizing Google Drive for storage and Google Colab for compute).

Data Engineering & Big Data Challenges

Data Ingestion and Preprocessing Constraints

The data ingestion pipeline involved aggregating 60 individually compressed user files into a unified tensor.

Observed Runtime Behaviour: Contrary to initial "Big Data" volume concerns, the complete dataset (377,000 rows) was successfully loaded into memory using a direct concatenation strategy (pd.concat). The primary constraint observed was not storage capacity, but I/O Latency. Reading and parsing the file structure required significant overhead compared to a monolithic binary format, resulting in an ingestion time of approximately 45 seconds for just 60 users.

System Limitation: While the 12GB RAM environment was sufficient, linear projection indicates that the pd.concat approach would hit a hard memory ceiling at approximately 600 users, validating the need for distributed processing in future production deployments.

Solutions: A NVIDIA Tesla T4 GPU runtime was used instead of the default Intel Xeon CPU runtime offered in Google Colab.  T4 GPU is widely used for deep learning tasks, especially for model inference. The T4 GPU is based on the Turing architecture and offers significant computational power.

To reduce the system load, the csv file in each compressed user file was extracted beforehand and loaded onto storage of the runtime, this proactive activity significantly reduced the time required to decompress the files during actual analysis.

Additionally, the files were uploaded in batches  with some selected features based on most important features for authentication and  domain knowledge for behavioural biometrics to reduce system load as shown if the figure below.

Data imbalance, sparsity, noise, or missingness

The most significant statistical challenge was the extreme skew in class distribution and sensor availability.

Missingness Analysis To determine the degree of dataset integrity, a missing data analysis was conducted on the full feature set (d = 225). The analysis revealed that two location-based features (location:min_speed and location:max_speed) exhibited 72.3% missingness, significantly exceeding the 50% tolerance threshold. Consequently, these were dropped, and the feature space was pruned to 101 high-quality features. The figure below shows the results of the analysis.

Imbalance and Sparsity Analysis: The dataset exhibitsed a severe 1:30.5 imbalance ratio, with only 11,996 (3.18%) samples belonging to the target "Legitimate" class compared to 365,350 (96.82%) "Impostor" samples. A naive baseline model achieved 96.8% accuracy simply by predicting the majority class but failed to detect a single legitimate transition (Recall = 0.0). Furthermore, systematic missingness was observed, with 15.3% of records lacking Watch Accelerometer data due to device charging cycles.

Strategy Applied (SMOTE & Imputation): Median imputation was applied to the 101 selected features, successfully resolving 100% of the remaining NaN values.

SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) was implemented to upsample the minority class. This balanced the training distribution to 1:1, enabling the final Gradient Boosting model to achieve 99.26% Accuracy with a False Reject Rate (FRR) of only 7.09%, proving the model successfully learned the minority class patterns rather than collapsing into a majority-class predictor.

Question 3

Feature Engineering

To enable robust Hysteresis-based authentication, the feature engineering strategy focused on features could be processed with minimal system load which included transforming raw, noisy sensor streams into stable, orientation-invariant representations.  This process involved two distinct representation techniques followed by an automated selection phase using Random Forest. However due to the values of the features will be somewhat unavailable or could be spiked due to the real-life implementation, the techniques was important to maintain the integrity of that dataset.

Physics-Based Representation (Signal Magnitude)

Raw accelerometer and gyroscope data are typically provided as 3-axis vectors (x, y, z). These Cartesian coordinates were already computed into a single Signal Magnitude Vector (SMV) using the Euclidean norm: SMV =  by the authors (Vaizman et al.), (e,g [raw_acc:magnitude_stats:mean] and [raw_acc:magnitude_stats:percentile25])

In real-world authentication, users hold their phones in infinite orientations when typing, taking pictures, reading, to mention few. Raw accelerometer and gyroscope values are highly sensitive to device rotation. The Magnitude computed with Euclidean norm is rotation-invariant, providing a stable signal of the user's intensity of motion regardless of device posture. This stability is a helpful for the Hysteresis "Time-to-Trigger" logic, which requires consistent signal inputs to avoid false state transitions.

Statistical Distribution Embeddings

Instead of feeding raw time-series data, each 20-second window was represented as a vector of statistical moments: Mean, Standard Deviation and Skewness which were distinct features within the dataset. Implementation was cleaning them and scaling them (Mean Imputation) so the model could use them. Examples are [proc_gyro:3d:std_z] and [raw_acc:3d:mean_z].

Random Forest Gini Importance

To rigorously select the most discriminative features put of 103 features (manually selected features valid for authentication), Random Forest Classifier specifically for feature ranking (distinct from the final Gradient Boosting predictor) was used.

Methodology

Trained a Random Forest on the 103-feature set which included the signal magnitudes and statical distributions and calculated the Gini Importance (Mean Decrease in Impurity) for each feature.

The top 52 features which cumulatively captured 95% of the total information gain. Features below this threshold (contributing less than 0.1% importance) were discarded as noise.

The dominance of Standard Deviation (Rank 1) and Magnitude (Rank 3, 4) in the Random Forest ranking empirically justifies the domain-based decision to use these representations. By filtering the dataset to these top 52 features, we reduced the model's computational overhead while achieving a final accuracy of 99.26%, validating that the selected features contained the necessary signal for high-fidelity authentication. The 53-feature set were the most effective for the Hysteresis model, outperforming the entire over 200 available features.

These high-quality features will smoothen out transient noise spikes that would otherwise trigger the "Ping-Pong" effect in the authentication model. The image shows the

